    "summary": "The statement takes a conditional pro position: free central bus lines are acceptable only when two safeguards hold — the fare revenue that disappears is backfilled by a durable, non-volatile funding source, and free riding is bundled with service improvements (frequency, reliability, quality) plus targeted help for low-income riders. Read charitably, it says that fare abolition is a demand-side tool that fails on its own: without replacement revenue the operator degrades, and without better service the new free riders are simply offered a worse product, while the poorest riders may still be underserved if the network itself does not reach them. It therefore prefers a sequenced or conditional reform over an unconditional fare-zero mandate.",
