  "critic": {
    "summary": "The main line of attack is that the 'only if' clause is close to unfalsifiable in practice: no mid-sized city can guarantee 'stable public funding' in perpetuity, so the condition can be used to block fare-free transit indefinitely while appearing to support it. A second attack is that the bundle is over-specified relative to the question asked: the question is whether central lines should be free, and the statement answers a different, compound question about funding and service design. A third attack is empirical and cost-based: if the goal is helping low-income riders, means-tested discounts or targeted subsidies may deliver the same equity benefit at a fraction of the revenue loss, so universal free fares are not obviously the efficient instrument, and pairing free fares with frequency upgrades may strain the same operating budget twice. Note: no attack text was supplied with this node, so this is a PLAUSIBLE_COUNTER reconstructed from the position itself rather than a REAL_ATTACK.",
